% ============================================================================
% animal-adaptation — a named animal and the ONE survival adaptation it is
% known for (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Animals survive their environments through adaptations — traits or
% behaviors that help them cope with conditions like cold, scarce winter
% food, or long seasonal journeys. `animal_adaptation(animal, adaptation)`
% names one specific animal and the ONE adaptation it is known for. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? animal_adaptation(arctic_fox, $A)   % camouflage
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `animal_adaptation(animal, adaptation)`, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the adaptation WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on an animal not
% one of the three the cited pages name. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind an adaptation and recall the animal known for it:
%
%     ? animal_adaptation($X, hibernation)   % groundhog
%
% Each row's animal is a DIFFERENT species and each names a DIFFERENT kind
% of adaptation (a physical trait, a seasonal behavior, and a travel
% behavior), so the table's own citation is the PRIMARY/first-listed
% source below (mirroring `ocean-observing-instruments.adj`'s established
% multi-source discipline — an ADJ `table` carries only ONE table-level
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` block), and each OTHER row's own distinct
% citation is documented here in prose:
%
%     animal        adaptation    source page                                                  quote
%     ------------  ------------  ------------------------------------------------------------  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     arctic_fox    camouflage    nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/arctic-fox        "Arctic foxes have beautiful white (sometimes blue-gray) coats that act as very effective winter camouflage."
%     groundhog     hibernation   nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/groundhog          "After roughly three months of hibernation, evidence suggests that male groundhogs wake up early to prepare for the mating season."
%     canada_goose  migration     nationalgeographic.com/animals/birds/facts/canada-goose         "When the birds do migrate, they form impressive and aerodynamic 'V-formations.'"
%
% All three citations WebFetch-verified before writing, each with an
% explicit keyword search confirming the adaptation term appears in a
% clean quotable sentence on its own page. `trust consensus` for all
% three — nationalgeographic.com's main animal-facts pages are a
% reputable, widely-cited science publisher (not a primary .gov source),
% the same tier this stdlib already reserves for other National Geographic
% sources (`animal-habitat.adj`, `rainforest-layer.adj`). This is a
% DIFFERENT source family from an earlier, unsuccessful attempt to cite
% NPS teacher-lesson-plan pages for this same topic, which turned out to
% be activity prompts rather than pages that state concrete animal-to-
% adaptation facts. An animal not one of these three — a penguin, for
% example, real and well-known for its own adaptations, but not one this
% specific set of cited pages names here — has no row, so a recall on it
% ABSTAINS rather than inventing an adaptation. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table animal_adaptation {
    columns animal, adaptation

    row (arctic_fox, camouflage)
    row (groundhog, hibernation)
    row (canada_goose, migration)

    source "Arctic foxes have beautiful white (sometimes blue-gray) coats that act as very effective winter camouflage."
    locator "https://www.nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/arctic-fox"
    trust consensus
}
